### BBC Event Plane Calculation

The plane formed by the beam axis and the impact parameter is known as the reaction plane. Experimentally, it impossible to find the true reaction plane in any given collision [37], so we approximate it with the observed event plane calculated from the anisotropy of produced particles. At \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}\) up to 39 GeV, the BBC detectors offer a reasonably good event plane resolution. The inner two rings of the east and west BBC are used to calculate the event plane. The BBC and TPC + TOF are separated by a large \(\eta\) gap. This \(\eta\) gap ensures there is minimal non-flow contribution to the flow calculations in \(|\eta|\leq 1.0\). The \(n^{\rm th}\) harmonic event plane vector, \(\vec{Q}_{n}\), is defined by the equations

\[Q_{n,x}=\sum_{i}w_{i}\cos(n\phi_{i})=Q_{n}\cos(n\Psi_{n}) \tag{4.4}\]

Figure 4.7: On the left is shown the west and east BBC detectors, for positive and negative \(\eta\), respectively. On the right is shown the inner two rings of the BBC. The red numeral is tile number and blue numeral is the PMT number; these two are not always the same, since the read-out of tiles 7 and 9, and of 13 and 15, are coupled together.

\[Q_{n,y}=\sum_{i}w_{i}\sin(n\phi_{i})=Q_{n}\sin(n\Psi_{n}) \tag{4.5}\]

\[w_{i}=\frac{A_{i}}{\sum A_{i}} \tag{4.6}\]

Here, \(\phi_{i}\) is the azimuth of the center of the \(i^{\rm th}\) tile of the BBC, and \(w_{i}\) the normalized and gain-corrected ADC value (the equivalent of energy deposition) in the \(i^{\rm th}\) tile. The normalization for the east and west sides of the BBC is set independently.

The event plane angle \(\Psi_{n}\) for the \(n^{\rm th}\) harmonic is calculated using

\[\Psi_{n}=\frac{\tan^{-1}(Q_{n,y}/Q_{n,x})}{n} \tag{4.7}\]

The imperfect azimuthal symmetry of the TPC (affected by imperfections such as sector boundaries, non-uniform efficiency, temporarily dead channels, etc.) makes the reconstructed event plane distribution non-uniform (not-flat), and could bias flow measurements if not corrected [90]. Figure 4.8 shows a 1st-order raw event plane distribution for BES energies. To correct this non-uniformity, three methods are widely used, namely phi weighting, re-centering, and shifting. In this dissertation, the shifting method is used.

#### Shift Correction

In the shift correction method [91], a correction term is chosen to force the \(n^{\rm th}\) Fourier harmonic in the raw event plane distribution, \(\Psi\), to become flat. The raw event plane distribution can be expanded in a Fourier series as follows:

\[\frac{dN}{d\Psi}=\frac{a_{0}}{2}+\sum_{n}\left(a_{n}\cos n\Psi+b_{n}\sin n\Psi\right) \tag{4.8}\]

where coefficients \(a_{n}\) and \(b_{n}\) are

\[a_{n}=\frac{1}{\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\frac{dN}{d\Psi}\cos n\Psi d\Psi,\;\;\;n=0, 1,2... \tag{4.9}\]

Figure 4.8: Raw event plane distribution for east, west and full event plane calculations.

