directed flow. This hypothesis is suggested by the observation that the measured \(v_{1}\) for \(\pi^{+}\) and \(\pi^{-}\)[49], for \(K^{+}\) and \(K^{-}\), for \(\bar{p}\) and \(\bar{\Lambda}\) are very similar. Therefore, the following definition of net-proton \(v_{1}\) is our best estimate of a proxy observable for the transported component,

\[[v_{1}(y)]_{p}=r(y)[v_{1}(y)]_{\bar{p}}+(1-r(y))\,[v_{1}(y)]_{\rm net \mbox{-}p}\,, \tag{5.1}\]

where \(r(y)\) is the rapidity-dependent ratio of observed antiprotons to protons. Then we define net-\(\Lambda\) and net-kaon \(v_{1}\) using the same formula, with \(p\) and \(\bar{p}\) replaced by \(\Lambda\) and \(\bar{\Lambda}\) for net-\(\Lambda\) flow and \(p\) and \(\bar{p}\) replaced by \(K^{+}\) and \(K^{-}\) for net-kaon flow, respectively. Figure 5.16 shows net-proton, net-\(\Lambda\) and net-kaon results. The net-particle \(dv_{1}/dy\) results are consistent for protons and \(\Lambda\)s, but \(dv_{1}/dy\) for net kaons strongly deviates from the other two cases for beam energies less than 14.5 GeV. The reason for the latter pattern is not yet understood.

